## 🧠 Detection Engine (ML & NLP Pipeline)

The engine runs **two models**, each trained on the signal it can actually learn, and combines them.

### 1. URL model: Random Forest + XGBoost ensemble
17 lexical features taken mainly from the **hostname**, the part of the address an attacker controls (`backend/features.py`):

| Feature | What it catches |
| :--- | :--- |
| `Suspicious_TLD` | High-risk endings such as `.top`, `.xyz`, `.tk`, `.click` |
| `Brand_Impersonation` | Brand name on a domain the brand doesn't own (`maybank2u-secure-login.com`) |
| `Brand_Lookalike` | Typosquats and homoglyphs (`rnaybank2u.com`, `g00gle.com`) |
| `Brand_In_Path` | `/maybank/login.php` hosted on an unrelated or hacked site |
| `Is_IP_Host`, `Has_At_Symbol`, `Is_Punycode` | Raw-IP hosts, `real.com@evil.top` tricks, homograph domains |
| `Is_URL_Shortener`, `Free_Hosting` | `bit.ly` and similar shorteners, `*.web.app`, `*.weebly.com` and similar free hosts |
| `Uses_HTTP` | Connection without TLS |
| `Phishy_Host_Words`, `Suspicious_Path` | `login`, `verify`, `semakan`, `kemaskini`… in the hostname, or `.php` login kits in the path |
| `Hostname_Length`, `Num_Subdomains`, `Hostname_Hyphens`, `Hostname_Digits`, `Host_Entropy` | Structure of the hostname |

> **Why there is no `URL_Length > 75` rule:** legitimate URLs for Gmail, Google Search, Shopee products, and bank portals are often over 75 characters because of paths and tracking parameters. Total URL length is not a reliable phishing signal, so only the hostname is measured.

### 2. Text model: TF-IDF + Logistic Regression and the Threat Dictionary
* The page text is split into sentences. Each sentence that uses enough known Malay vocabulary is scored by the text model.
* Phrases in `THREAT_DICTIONARY` (Bahasa Malaysia, Manglish, English) are combined with a noisy-OR rule, so several phrases strengthen each other while a single phrase stays moderate.

### 3. Threat intelligence, behaviour & certificate checks (`backend/threat_rules.py`)
| Check | Example it catches |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Offline blocklist lookup**: 5.2M known-bad hostnames from public feeds (`database/threat_intel.db`) | `bintv.cc` (malware feed), known gambling and piracy domains |
| **Drive-by downloads**: the extension reports files the site actually downloaded (`chrome.downloads`) | `jtp-photography.com` silently downloading a `Setup.exe` |
| **Malvertising redirects**: ad-network URL parameters (`zoneid`, `cost`, `clickid`, `subid`…) | Pop-under landing pages |
| **Online gambling**: Malay/English betting terms from `THREAT_DICTIONARY` (category *Gambling*) | `mega888`, `slot online`, `kredit percuma`, `sportsbook` |
| **Illegal streaming**: "free in HD / no sign-up" phrases plus sports or movie context | Free cricket/football streams full of malicious ads |
| **Fake alerts**: tech-support scams and "click Allow" notification tricks | "Your computer is infected, call Microsoft Support" |
| **Credential forms**: a password field on HTTP, or a form that posts to another site | Fake login pages |
| **HTTPS certificate**: a real TLS handshake with chain and hostname verification | Expired, self-signed or wrong-host certificates |

#### How HTTPS is checked
The address bar only shows that a page *claims* `https://`. The engine actually connects to the site and verifies the certificate chain against the Windows trust store plus Mozilla's root bundle (`certifi`). It also checks the hostname, expiry date and issuer. A **valid certificate only means the connection is encrypted, not that the site is honest**: most phishing sites have free valid certificates. So a valid certificate never lowers the risk score; an *invalid* one raises it.

### 4. Fusion
* `Risk = max(URL score, content score, strongest behaviour/blocklist finding)`. Add +10 when at least two engines are ≥ 50%.
* The URL score is **calibrated** for real-world prevalence. A raw model score of 80 maps to the Danger threshold of 70; only 1.3% of unseen legitimate sites score that high.
* Very popular sites (Tranco top 10k / 100k) have their URL-only score reduced.
* **Official domains** (e.g. `google.com`, `maybank2u.com.my`, `*.gov.my`) never reach *Danger*. If the page *shows* scam content, such as a phishing email open in Gmail, the result is capped at **Medium Risk** with a warning.
* The **threat type** shown (Phishing, Online Gambling, Drive-by Download, Illegal Streaming…) comes from concrete evidence first, and from the URL model's guess only when there is none.

### 5. Evaluation
| Test | Result |
| :--- | :--- |
| URL model, held-out domains (malicious vs legit) | **85.7% accuracy** (gambling F1 0.89, piracy 0.81, phishing 0.74) |
| Text model, unseen sentence templates | **97.0% accuracy** |
| Scenario suite (`python backend/test_scan_scenarios.py`) | **33/33**: 13 safe, 3 medium, 17 high-risk sites |
| 2,000 unseen legit Tranco domains, URL only | 1.3% Danger |
| 2,000 *new* phishing URLs not on any blocklist, URL only | 72% flagged (Medium or above); content and behaviour checks add more |

